  "text": "**Solution 2 is the best of the three, and its value checks out numerically.**\n\n**Solution 2's polynomial.** With $a=d=1$, $b=c=e^{i\\beta}$, $\\cos\\beta = A = 2-\\sqrt5$, the factorization $f=(z+1)(z^2+(e^{i\\beta}-1)z+1)$ gives $|f(e^{i\\theta})|^2 = 2(1+t)\\big[(2t-1+A)^2+1-A^2\\big]$ with $t=\\cos\\theta$. Evaluating this:\n- At $t=1$: $|f(1)|^2 = 8(1+A) = 8(3-\\sqrt5) \\approx 6.111$, so $|f(1)| \\approx 2.472 = 2(\\sqrt5-1)$.\n- Near $t\\approx-0.38$ there is a second local maximum with the same value (about $6.111$), so the two peaks equioscillate as claimed.\n- The remaining values I sampled are smaller, so the maximum is $2(\\sqrt5-1)\\approx 2.472$.\n\nThis is strictly below $\\approx 2.661$, the value Solutions 1 and 3 report, so their claimed minimum cannot be right. Both of those also use the same polynomial $z^3 - z^2 + z + 1$, which is not optimal.\n\n**Solution 2's optimality.** I have not proven that $2(\\sqrt5-1)$ is the global minimum over all four phases. The equioscillation (three active peaks, two free phase parameters) is consistent with a local minimum. I could not rule out a lower value by hand, so the answer is plausible rather than certain.\n\nCHOICE: 2",
